  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-50467",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t{tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{3}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
